<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Why time has an arrow</p>
    <h2 id="t-arrow">The Past Was Special</h2>
    <p>Microscopic equations are often reversible while macroscopic records are not. Statistical mechanics
      explains the overwhelmingly likely direction of change given an extraordinarily low-entropy boundary
      condition in the past. It does not yet explain why that boundary condition obtained. Cosmology calls
      this the Past Hypothesis problem.</p>
    <p>TTI&rsquo;s <a href="/seams/#t-arrow">geometric arrow</a> and
      <a href="/temporal-antipodes/">Temporal Antipodes</a> offer metaphysical pictures of that boundary.
      They do not replace the probability measure, the microscopic dynamics or the low-entropy initial
      condition. A picture becomes physics only when it reproduces those ingredients and risks a contrary
      observation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">TTI &middot; retained proposal</p>
      <h2 id="t-integration-phases">Phases as Levels of Integration</h2>
      <p>The transferred EGregoRA essay proposed a ladder from possibility, through partial coherence, to
        durable matter and reflective integration. TTI retains the useful question: can one define an order
        parameter \(I\) that measures coordinated causal participation rather than merely naming it?</p>
      <p>A credible \(I\) would have units or be explicitly dimensionless, remain invariant under harmless
        changes of description, specify the system boundary, and predict something not already built into its
        definition. Until then, &ldquo;phase&rdquo; is philosophical language, not a discovered phase of matter.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Historical material &middot; preserved, not promoted</p>
      <h2 id="t-symbolic-poles">101, 333, 666 and 999</h2>
      <p>The earlier page arranged these numbers as symbolic poles of initiation, formation, material
        completion and return. Their repeating digits, and the fact that \(666=T_{36}\), can support a
        cultural or numerological reading. They do not follow from Boltzmann entropy, quantum field theory or
        cosmological data.</p>
      <p>The arithmetic material belongs with the <a href="/spiral/">triangular-number spiral</a> and
        <a href="/skeletal-splice/">Skeletal Splice</a>, where exact identities can be checked before a
        cosmological meaning is considered. Symbolic resonance is retained here as history of the framework,
        not entered as physical evidence.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A statistical idea in everyday language</p>
    <h2 id="t-cards">Start with Possibilities, Not with “Disorder”</h2>
    <p>Take a shuffled deck of cards. One exact order is no more or less probable than any other exact order,
      so entropy cannot simply mean that one sequence looks untidy. The difference appears when many exact
      arrangements are grouped into a coarse description. “Every suit together in rank order” describes very
      few arrangements. “Thoroughly mixed” describes enormously many. A system is likely to move into the
      second kind of macrostate because there are more microscopic ways to realise it.</p>
    <p>Boltzmann&rsquo;s formula \(S=k_{\mathrm B}\ln\Omega\) expresses that count. \(\Omega\) is the number of
      microstates compatible with the chosen macrostate, and the logarithm makes entropy additive for
      independent systems. The formula has meaning only after the system boundary, macroscopic variables and
      counting rule have been declared. Change the coarse-graining and the entropy description may change.</p>
    <p>“Disorder” is sometimes a useful shortcut, but it easily misleads. A well-stacked library can have more
      thermodynamic entropy than a scattered cold pile of books because molecular energy, not visual neatness,
      dominates the count. Conversely, a turbulent pattern may look disorderly while carrying organised flows.
      The technical question is always: which states are being counted, under which constraints?</p>
    <p class="lede">Entropy measures multiplicity or uncertainty in a defined description. It is not a cosmic
      score for messiness, moral decline or lack of meaning.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Order can grow locally</p>
    <h2 id="t-open-systems">Life Does Not Violate the Second Law</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A refrigerator</h3>
      <p>The inside becomes colder and more constrained, but the motor releases more heat into the room. The
        refrigerator is an open system. Its local entropy reduction is paid for by a larger increase in its
        surroundings, so total entropy does not decrease.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A living organism</h3>
      <p>Cells maintain intricate organisation by taking in low-entropy free energy and releasing heat and
        waste. Growth, repair and memory are compatible with the second law because the organism continually
        exchanges matter and energy with its environment.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Gravity</h3>
      <p>A cloud can clump into stars and galaxies even while total entropy rises. Gravitating systems behave
        differently from a gas in a fixed box; radiation, heating and black-hole entropy matter. Visual
        structure is not a reliable entropy meter.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>TTI integration</h3>
      <p>If integration means stronger functional coordination, it may increase while thermodynamic entropy
        also increases, or while entropy is exported. No universal minus sign links them. Their relationship
        must be derived for each defined system. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A calculation small enough to inspect</p>
    <h2 id="t-bit-example">One Bit Shows Why Probability Matters</h2>
    <p>Suppose a variable has two outcomes. If one outcome is certain, the Shannon entropy is zero: learning
      the result provides no surprise. If both outcomes have probability one-half, uncertainty is maximal for
      that two-state variable:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      H=-\sum_i p_i\log_2p_i
      =-2\left(\frac12\log_2\frac12\right)=1\ \text{bit}.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is an informational quantity. It becomes thermodynamic only through a physical implementation and
      protocol. A bit may be carried by a voltage, magnetic domain, photon polarisation or many other systems,
      each with energetic constraints. Erasing a bit at temperature \(T\) has a minimum thermodynamic cost in
      the ideal Landauer limit, but the abstract bit is not itself a packet of heat.</p>
    <p>Quantum von Neumann entropy makes a related distinction. A pure state has zero von Neumann entropy even
      when some measurements have uncertain outcomes. A subsystem entangled with another can have positive
      entropy although the combined state is pure. “Unknown to us”, “mixed because of preparation”, and
      “entangled with an environment” must not be collapsed into one story.</p>
    <p class="lede">Information can be rigorously quantified, but the word alone does not identify a physical
      carrier, an energy budget or a conscious experience.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Three possible relationships</p>
    <h2 id="t-integration-relations">Integration and Entropy Need Not Move in Opposite Directions</h2>
    <p>First, integration could rise while a subsystem&rsquo;s entropy falls. A controlled process may narrow the
      subsystem&rsquo;s available states and coordinate its parts, exporting entropy to the environment. Second,
      integration and entropy could rise together. A learning network or developing organism may acquire more
      internal differentiation, more correlations and access to a larger set of microstates while becoming
      functionally more coherent. Third, one quantity may change while the other remains largely independent.
      A gas can equilibrate without becoming an integrated agent.</p>
    <p>These possibilities show why a bridge law needs two independently defined axes. Let \(I\) measure some
      operational feature such as causal influence, coordinated response or irreducible correlation. Let
      \(S\) be a named thermodynamic or informational entropy. Data could then determine whether
      \(dI/dt\) and \(dS/dt\) are positively related, negatively related or unrelated under controlled
      conditions. Defining \(I\) as “the opposite of entropy” would build the desired conclusion into the
      vocabulary and make the claim impossible to test.</p>
    <p>The cosmological arrow adds another requirement. Microscopic laws can be largely time-reversal
      symmetric while entropy increases toward what we call the future because the observable universe began
      in a remarkably low-entropy macrostate. TTI may ask why that boundary was special. Assigning two
      infinities to temporal antipodes is not yet an answer unless it yields the low-entropy condition and the
      subsequent statistical behaviour.</p>
    <p>Coarse-graining is not an admission that entropy is merely subjective. Experimental access and physical
      constraints determine meaningful macrostates, and thermodynamic predictions can be extraordinarily
      objective and precise. The caution is that a calculation must state its resolution. Counting molecular
      arrangements, logical messages and conscious reports are three different tasks even when all use a
      logarithm.</p>
    <p>The direction of entropy increase also depends on the system being sufficiently isolated and on a
      low-entropy boundary condition. Fluctuations can temporarily lower entropy in small systems, while the
      statistical law remains overwhelmingly reliable at macroscopic scale. The second law is not an absolute
      ban on every local decrease; it is a quantitative statement about typical evolution and total accounting.</p>
    <p>For TTI, this opens a more precise research question than “is integration order?” One can ask whether a
      defined integration measure predicts work extraction, heat flow, correlation structure or relaxation
      time after established variables are controlled. If it predicts none of them, it may still organise a
      philosophical account of wholeness, but it is not yet a thermodynamic state function.</p>
    <p>A concrete first study could compare two preparations of the same open system. Investigators would
      measure energy and entropy flow using established methods while calculating an integration score chosen
      before the trial. Repeating the protocol across driving strengths would reveal whether the score carries
      information not already contained in temperature, correlations or relaxation time. Held-out conditions
      would test prediction rather than retrospective description.</p>
    <p>If one function linked the measures across those conditions, the bridge would gain empirical content. If
      the relationship changed freely with every setup, integration would remain a context-dependent label.
      Either outcome would clarify the framework more than treating every organised pattern as evidence.</p>
    <p class="muted">The accessible verdict is not that entropy defeats the project. It narrows the project&rsquo;s
      honest claim: integration is a proposed organising variable whose connection to established entropy
      measures remains to be constructed and tested. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A derivation gate</p>
    <h2 id="t-bridge">What a Real Entropy&ndash;Integration Bridge Would Need</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>State space:</strong> define the microstates, macrostates and system boundary.</li>
      <li><strong>Dynamics:</strong> state how probabilities evolve and whether the evolution is reversible.</li>
      <li><strong>Order parameter:</strong> define integration independently of the effect it is meant to explain.</li>
      <li><strong>Bridge law:</strong> derive a relation such as \(I=F[\rho]\), rather than relying on verbal resemblance.</li>
      <li><strong>Null models:</strong> compare against ordinary equilibration, self-organisation and gravitational clumping.</li>
      <li><strong>Loss condition:</strong> name a measurement that could show the proposed bridge is absent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="lede">Without those six items, the safe statement is that entropy and integration are two
      descriptions that may move together, move oppositely or be independent, depending on the system.</p>
  </div>
</section>

